.TH "UBERTOOTH" "7" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "Project Ubertooth Overview"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\fR \- Project Ubertooth
.SH "OVERVIEW"
Ubertooth is a platform for Bluetooth experimentation\. It is able to sniff Bluetooth Smart (BLE), discover undiscoverable classic Bluetooth devices, and perform rudimentary sniffing of classic Bluetooth devices\.
.P
Ubertooth is \fBnot\fR a Bluetooth dongle and it will not show up as one when running \fBhciconfig\fR\.
.SH "COMMAND LINE TOOLS"
The following command line tools included in this package are the primary means of using Ubertooth\. Refer to their manual pages for example usage and comprehensive documentation\.
.P
Useful commands:
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-btle(1) : BLE sniffing and other fun
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-rx(1) : Classic Bluetooth device discovery and rudimentary sniffing
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-scan(1) : Active (BlueZ) scanning of undiscoverable devices; Linux only
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-afh(1) : Detecting the AFH map of a Classic Bluetooth piconet
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-ego(1) : Yuneec E\-Go electric skateboard sniffing
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-follow(1) : Active (BlueZ) clock discovery and piconet following; Linux only
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-btbr(1) : Experimental Classic Bluetooth connections and monitoring; needs the separate \fBbtbr\fR firmware image
.IP "" 0
.P
Utility commands:
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-dfu(1) : Firmware update tool
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-dump(1) : Dumping raw RF symbols to disk, Bluetooth or generic (experimental)
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-tx(1) : Transmitting a generic packet (experimental), and retransmitting symbols dumped with ubertooth\-dump
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-ducky(1) : Driving an Uberducky as a BLE USB Rubber Ducky
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-util(1) : "Everything else"
.IP "" 0
.P
Less useful commands:
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-debug(1) : Reading registers on the CC2400, and writing them (experimental)
.IP "\(bu" 4
ubertooth\-specan(1) : Raw RSSI values used by graphical specan
.IP "" 0
.SH "COMMON OPTIONS"
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR mean the same thing in every tool, including the three written in Python \-\- ubertooth\-btbr(1), \fBbtle\-extcap\.py\fR and ubertooth\-specan\-ui(1) \-\- which take their command lines from getopt in Python, from Wireshark's extcap protocol and from \fBargparse\fR\. Those three answer \fB\-V\fR by running ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-V\fR and passing its answer on, so the lines below are the same words whichever tool was asked; \fBubertooth\-util\fR has to be on their \fBPATH\fR, as it is when the tools are installed together, and one that is not there is reported as \fBerror: could not run ubertooth\-util: No such file or directory\fR\. \fBbtle\-extcap\.py\fR has no \fB\-U\fR, so its \fB\-V\fR reports every attached board\. ubertooth\-specan\-ui(1) answers \fB\-V\fR and \fB\-h\fR without the \fBnumpy\fR and Qt binding its window needs, and ubertooth\-btbr(1) answers them without the Python \fBlibusb1\fR package a session of its own needs\. \fB\-v\fR is not in every tool; the last paragraph here says what it means where it is\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR prints that tool's usage text on standard output and exits 0\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR prints the version on standard output, and is the way to ask what is installed and what a board is running\. It exits 0, except for the two failures below: a board that is attached and cannot be read, and a USB bus that cannot be listed at all\. Every tool answers it in the same words, one labelled line each, so a script can take the line it wants whichever tool it ran:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Host tools version: libubertooth <version> (<revision>), libbtbb <version> (<revision>)
libbtbb loaded from: <path>
Firmware version: <image> <revision> (API:<version>)
Firmware build: <build string the board reports>
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The first two lines are the host's and need no board: the versions these tools were built from, and which libbtbb this run loaded, which is what tells a distribution or Homebrew build apart from this fork's\.
.P
The two firmware lines describe the board \fB\-U\fR names, or the only board attached\. With several boards attached and no \fB\-U\fR, both lines are printed for each of them with its index in the label, as the identity queries of ubertooth\-util(1) are: \fBFirmware version (0): \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR, \fBFirmware build (0): \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR\.
.P
With no Ubertooth attached the host lines are followed by \fBFirmware version: no Ubertooth attached\fR and the status is still 0: the host half of the answer needs no board, and nothing attached is an answer rather than a failure\. A board sitting in the DFU bootloader is named in a \fBnote:\fR on standard error there, because no tool can open one and that is the usual reason a board is not in the answer\. A board that is attached but cannot be read \-\- in use by another program, or not answering \-\- has the reason on its own line (\fBFirmware version: unavailable, in use by another program\fR, or \fBFirmware version: read failed\fR) and the status is 1\. So does a USB bus that cannot be listed at all, which is nothing known rather than nothing attached: \fBFirmware version: unavailable, the USB bus could not be listed\fR\.
.P
Part of an answer can go missing on its own, and the status is 1 then too: a board that answered its version but not its build string prints \fBFirmware build: read failed\fR under a good version line, or \fBFirmware build: not reported\fR if the read worked and the board had no build string to give, and one whose API version could not be read prints the version with no ` (API:\|\.\|\.\|\.)` after it\. So a script that cares about the whole answer should test the status, not only look for a reason in place of a version\.
.P
Asking for the version does not disturb a board: it is not told to stop, so a mode running on it keeps running, and any firmware is read whatever API version it reports, which is what makes \fB\-V\fR the way to identify an unknown board\.
.P
\fB\-v\fR means verbose, where a tool has verbose output: ubertooth\-specan(1) \fB\-v\fR, ubertooth\-debug(1) \fB\-v <0\-2>\fR, and ubertooth\-btle(1) \fB\-v\fR, which describes each packet whose data the decoder could not read\. One tool spells something else \fB\-v\fR, an older spelling kept so that existing scripts keep working: ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-v\fR prints the \fBFirmware version:\fR line on its own (deprecated, and unchanged: use \fB\-V\fR)\. ubertooth\-btle(1) \fB\-v\fR named that tool's CRC verify setting until \fB\-k\fR took it over, so a value written after it is refused with a line naming \fB\-k\fR\.
.SH "OPTION VALUES"
These pages write an option's value as a separate word: \fBubertooth\-util \-c 2440\fR, \fBubertooth\-btle \-a 8e89bed6\fR, \fBubertooth\-rx \-l 446688 \-u 22\fR\. This follows a standard, the POSIX Utility Syntax Guidelines (guideline 6: an option and its value are separate arguments)\. The value placeholders are written the same way (\fB\-c <2402\-2480>\fR)\. The older attached form is still accepted for backward compatibility with existing scripts, so \fB\-c 2440\fR and \fB\-c2440\fR are the same\. Where the value is optional, the next word is taken as the value only when it is a valid value, so an option never swallows the option after it: \fB\-c \-b\fR reads the channel and then the board ID, while \fB\-z \-90\fR sets the squelch to \-90 dBm\. The \fBubertooth\-*\fR tools written in C take single\-letter options only, and refuse a long one with \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-\-bogus': the tools take single\-letter options\fR, and so does ubertooth\-btbr(1)\. The long options of the two tools that have them \-\- \fBbtle\-extcap\.py\fR, whose are Wireshark's, and ubertooth\-specan\-ui(1) \fB\-\-wifi\fR and \fB\-\-padding\fR \-\- take \fB\-\-name value\fR or \fB\-\-name=value\fR, never attached without the \fB=\fR\. A lone \fB\-\fR as the value of a file option means standard output, so \fBubertooth\-btle \-q \-\fR writes the capture to stdout\. No option reads its input from a lone \fB\-\fR: for those it is a file name like any other, and there is no such file\. Any other value is a file name too, including one that starts with \fB\-\fR, so a forgotten file name writes a file rather than guessing\. A destination whose writer seeks back over what it has written cannot be \fB\-\fR at all, which a pipe does not allow: the PcapNG captures (\fB\-r\fR) and \fBubertooth\-dfu \-u\fR refuse it and say what to use instead\. Standard output carries one stream, so two capture options cannot both have it, and the tool whose capture has it prints none of its own packet report there\.
.P
Options can be written together behind one dash (\fB\-f \-n\fR is \fB\-fn\fR), so a stray dash inside such a word is a letter the tools do not have\. The refusal names the word it was written in rather than the dash on its own: \fBubertooth\-util \-v\-\fR and \fBubertooth\-util \-r\-x\fR both read \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-' in '<word>'\fR\. The trailing form used to be blamed on \fB\-\-\fR, or on the option after it (\fBubertooth\-util \-v\- \-h\fR named \fB\-h\fR), neither of which the user had written\. Where several words hold a dash, the one named is the word the parse stopped in, which is the first word the tools would not take: \fBubertooth\-util \-v\- \-Ufoo\-\fR names \fB\-v\-\fR, because the dash in \fB\-Ufoo\-\fR is part of \fB\-U\fR's value and nothing in that word is wrong\.
.P
An option may be given a value only once\. \fB\-l 446688 \-l aabbcc\fR names two LAPs for one capture, so it is refused with \fBerror: option '\-l' given more than once\fR before any board is opened, rather than one of them being kept and the other dropped without a word\. Only a value counts: repeating an option that takes none says the same thing twice, and an option whose value is optional has two jobs, so \fBubertooth\-util \-c 2426 \-c\fR sets the channel and then reads it back\. A tool may still refuse a repeat of its own where the second one is more likely a typo than a request, and says so on its page: \fBubertooth\-specan \-v\fR is the one that does\.
.P
The rule covers every \fBubertooth\-*\fR tool\. \fBbtle\-extcap\.py\fR and \fBubertooth\-specan\-ui\fR take their command lines from Wireshark's extcap protocol and from \fBargparse\fR, and still keep the last value\.
.P
For the same reason, two options that cannot both run are refused with one \fBerror:\fR line: two major modes, two modulations for one capture, two output formats for one sweep, or the same setting written two ways (\fBubertooth\-util \-c 2440 \-C 24\fR)\. Each page lists its own\.
.SH "SELECTING A DEVICE"
With a single Ubertooth attached, the tools find it automatically\. With more than one attached, every tool takes \fB\-U <index|serial>\fR to choose one: an index is a position in the list of attached Ubertooths (\fB\-U 0\fR is the first), and a serial\-number string selects that board whatever the enumeration order\. An index that is not attached is refused (\fBerror: no Ubertooth at index 2 (2 attached)\fR) and the tool exits non\-zero\. Indexes are positions in the list of boards attached when the command runs, so they shift when a board is added or removed; to keep targeting the same board, select it by serial\.
.P
With several attached and no \fB\-U\fR, no board is selected: the tools refuse the command and then list the boards, so the next command can name one\. Reading those serials opens each board briefly, and none is kept open\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: multiple Ubertooth devices found; select one with \-U <index|serial>
  Device 0: aa1100112233445566778899aabbccdd
  Device 1: 19bb00112233445566778899aabbccdd
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
With none attached, every tool says \fBerror: no Ubertooth devices found\fR\.
.P
Selecting by serial number is the slower form, because every attached board is opened and asked for its serial, and a board that does not answer costs a second\. A Ctrl\-C during that search ends the run: the search gives up where it is, no board is selected, and the tool exits 0 without a message of its own, because the user stopped a run that had not yet asked anything of a board\. A Ctrl\-C between the board being opened and the first request made of it ends the run the same way\. A search that did finish and matched nothing still says so and exits 1, whether or not a signal arrived while it ran: the answer is true either way, and a script reading the status to learn whether its board is there must not be told that it is\.
.P
SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGQUIT and an alarm (SIGALRM, as \fBtimeout\fR and \fBkill\fR send them) end a run the same way Ctrl\-C does, which means exit 0 rather than death by the signal\. SIGHUP is caught even when it was inherited ignored, so a capture run under \fBnohup\fR stops when the terminal closes instead of carrying on with its mode left running on the board\.
.P
A serial may be shortened to its first few characters, in either case, so \fB\-U aa11\fR and \fB\-U AA11\fR select the same board\. If those characters start more than one attached board's serial, the tool lists the matching serials and selects neither\. An all\-digits string is read as an index, so include a non\-digit (\fB\-U 19b\fR rather than \fB\-U 19\fR)\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SELECTING A DEVICE, for the full description and for listing the attached devices\.
.SH "WHEN A BOARD CANNOT BE USED"
A board can be attached and still be unavailable, because only one program at a time can use it and because a mode keeps running on the board until it is stopped\. Both cases are reported in words, with the libusb code in parentheses for a bug report\.
.P
The board opened, and then something else turned out to be holding it:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: Ubertooth 19bb00112233445566778899aabbccdd is in use by another program (libusb \-3)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
That is another tool from this package, a graphical specan, or anything else holding its USB interface; on Linux it may be a kernel driver\. Stop that program and run the command again\. Which libusb code says so depends on the system, which is why the message does not depend on it\. Nothing is sent to the board on this path, so the command does not disturb whatever is using it\.
.P
With one board attached and no \fB\-U\fR, there is no name to use, and a \fB\-U <index>\fR run has only the index:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: the Ubertooth is in use by another program (libusb \-6)
error: Ubertooth at index 1 is in use by another program (libusb \-3)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The board took the request and sent nothing back:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: the Ubertooth did not answer in time (libusb \-7)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A board that has stopped answering control requests does this: it happens after some captures end, and a board left in a mode that keeps polling can do it too\. Try \fBubertooth\-util [\-U <selector>] \-S\fR to stop a mode and \fBubertooth\-util \-r\fR to reset the board; a board that answers neither usually resets itself, as the next paragraphs describe\. The line says only what happened, because what it means depends on what the command was doing: mid\-flash, for instance, it is a bootloader that lost its place, and no mode is involved\.
.P
A board still answering, left in a mode by a run that was killed, needs no \fB\-S\fR before most captures\. \fBubertooth\-rx\fR, \fBubertooth\-dump\fR, \fBubertooth\-scan\fR, \fBubertooth\-follow\fR, \fBubertooth\-afh\fR and \fBubertooth\-specan\fR read which mode the board is in as they start, stop a mode they find and wait for the board to report itself idle before they read\. They say so, because the capture they stopped was somebody's:
.IP "" 4
.nf
warning: the board is still in mode 9 from an earlier run; stopping it first (ubertooth\-util \-w names the modes)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Without that wait, the stopped mode could hand the board's receive endpoint one more packet before the new capture's own mode had started, and the capture would open with a packet or two of the earlier run's\. A board that does not reach idle within about a second is a second warning, and the capture goes ahead\.
.P
\fBubertooth\-btle\fR and \fBubertooth\-ego\fR read their packets a different way \(em one request per packet rather than the stream endpoint \(em so neither does this, and a board left in a mode is still theirs to meet: stop it with \fBubertooth\-util \-S\fR first\.
.P
A board running this tree's firmware usually recovers itself: it resets about three seconds after it stops servicing USB, drops off the bus and comes back idle, so the fix is to run the command again\. \fBubertooth\-util [\-U <selector>] \-w\fR then says how often that has happened and which mode and which wait the board was in the last time\.
.P
Not every board gets that self\-reset, and where it does not the fallback is always the same: unplug the board or power\-cycle its hub port\. That is what the \fBnote:\fR line the tools print after the error names (see ubertooth\-rx(1), EXIT STATUS)\. Firmware without the watchdog, such as \fB2020\-12\-R1\fR, has no self\-reset at all\. Neither has anything that feeds the watchdog from its loop rather than from a USB service pass \(em the six modes that service USB from the interrupt, and the \fBbtbr\fR image \(em because that loop keeps turning when the USB path alone has died (see ubertooth\-util(1), WATCHDOG)\. The bootloader runs with the watchdog off by design, so a \fB\-7\fR mid\-flash, where no mode is involved, waits for nothing either; ubertooth\-dfu(1) reports that one in its own words, and its page says the same about a board that answers nothing\.
.P
A serial\-number selector names the reason, because a board it could not read may be the board asked for:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: no Ubertooth found with serial matching 'aa1'
1 attached Ubertooth device did not answer the serial read
note: a board that does not answer may be in use by another program, or busy in a mode; stop a mode with ubertooth\-util \-U <index> \-S, or reset a board that answers nothing with \-r
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The remedy is by index there because a board whose serial could not be read cannot be selected by serial\. \fB\-r\fR on a board that answers nothing resets its USB connection before it asks the board to reset itself, because the board would refuse that request as it refuses every other one; the connection reset is what recovers it, and the board is then reset like any other\. See ubertooth\-util(1)\.
.P
A board in the DFU bootloader is named too, because it is not a board that went away: while it is being flashed it enumerates as the bootloader, which no tool but ubertooth\-dfu(1) can open, so every selection finds nothing until the flash finishes\. An interrupted flash leaves it there until the next one succeeds\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: no Ubertooth found with serial matching 'aa1'
note: a board is in the DFU bootloader, so no tool can open it: it is being flashed, or a flash left it there
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A board that cannot be \fIopened\fR at all is a different problem, usually file permissions: on Linux, install the udev rules shipped with this package (see ubertooth\-util(1), SELECTING A DEVICE)\.
.SH "FIRMWARE OF ANOTHER API VERSION"
These tools drive the firmware built from the same source tree, which both run\-time images report as their USB device version\. A release build refuses anything else, naming both versions and which side to replace:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: Ubertooth firmware API version <version> found, libubertooth 1\.1 requires <version>\.
note: flash the firmware built from the same source tree, for example: \./install\.sh \-\-flash\-device aa11
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A build made from a git checkout that is not on a tag is a development build, and says the same thing as a warning and carries on, because host tools and firmware from different commits are the normal state of lab work and debugging:
.IP "" 4
.nf
warning: Ubertooth firmware API version <version> found, libubertooth 1\.1 requires <version>\.
note: flash the firmware built from the same source tree, for example: \./install\.sh \-\-flash\-device aa11
note: this is a development build, so the command runs on; what it reports may be wrong, and a request this firmware has not got will fail
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Both notes are worth reading before the output is trusted\. What the two versions disagree about may be the layout of a record, in which case the capture is decoded by the wrong rules and is wrong rather than empty\. Where the firmware is simply missing a request, the command fails on it:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: could not set the channel: the board's firmware does not support it (stalled, libusb \-9)
note: the board reports firmware API version <version>, libubertooth 1\.1 requires <version>
note: a board that stalls every request is wedged rather than short of this one; reset it with ubertooth\-util \-r on the same board
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
That is the one wording for a request the board refuses, wherever the request came from\. The board answers such a request by stalling it and says nothing else, so the line names the request that was refused, and the first note puts the two versions beside each other: different versions name the reflash that fixes it, and equal versions say the version is not the problem\. The libusb code is there for a request sent and answered one at a time; a request sent without waiting for its answer has only the stall to report, so that line ends \fB(stalled)\fR\. A board in the DFU bootloader runs no application firmware, so ubertooth\-dfu(1) reports a refused request without either note\.
.P
The second note is for the other reason a request stalls, which the first cannot be told apart from: a wedged board stalls everything sent to it, whatever its firmware implements, so a report naming the firmware would send the user to reflash a board that is not short of the request at all\. The question that separates the two is whether any request gets through, and \fBubertooth\-util \-r\fR is the recovery for the wedge: it resets the board's USB connection and then the board, with no one at the bench\.
.P
A board that has left the USB bus stalls a request too, on some hosts, and its firmware is not the reason: the board whose version the note would quote is not there any more\. Before the firmware is blamed the bus is listed, and a board that has gone from it gets one line of its own, with no note:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: could not stop the running mode: the board is no longer attached
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Nothing is asked of the board to find that out, so a board that is merely silent is left alone\. A bus that cannot be listed says nothing either way, and the request is reported as the stall it arrived as\. So is one sent to a board whose version was never recorded, which is ubertooth\-dfu(1) talking to the bootloader: there is no handle there to ask the bus about\. Every board these tools open through libubertooth is recorded as it is opened, so every board a mode runs on is covered\.
.P
Which answer a build gives can be set either way with \fBUBERTOOTH_ALLOW_API_MISMATCH\fR (see ENVIRONMENT below)\.
.P
The version is only half of it: this tree builds two run\-time images, and they report the same version\. So the tools that check the version check the image as well, as the board is opened, and one carrying the other image is refused by name with the line that flashes it:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: the Ubertooth is running btbr; this tool needs bluetooth_rxtx
note: flash it with ubertooth\-dfu \-U aa11 \-d firmware/bluetooth_rxtx/bluetooth_rxtx\.dfu \-r
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBbtbr\fR answers the requests both images share and nothing else, so without that the command failed on the first request it does not answer, reported as a refused request \(em true, but about the wrong thing\. ubertooth\-btbr(1) is the tool that drives \fBbtbr\fR, and makes no such check: a board carrying \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR reaches its session and is reported from the write that times out there\. ubertooth\-util(1) and ubertooth\-dfu(1) check neither version nor image, because both have to work on whatever firmware a board is carrying\. ubertooth\-debug(1) checks the image and not the version: reading CC2400 registers is debugging work on whatever revision a board carries, but the other image answers no register read, so that board is named rather than left to fail on the read\.
.P
A board that will not say which image it runs is left to the request that needs it\. Two boards answer nothing there: one too busy to take the read, and one running firmware older than the info block, which a development build reaches because it reports the version mismatch and runs on\. Both are boards somebody means to use, and neither can be told from the other, so neither is refused\.
.SH "GENERIC RADIO MODE"
Experimental, and all of it is reachable from the command line now: \fBubertooth\-debug \-w\fR writes the radio's registers, \fBubertooth\-dump \-g\fR receives with them, and \fBubertooth\-tx \-g\fR transmits one packet\. Both halves of the receive work on our bench \-\- the symbol stream, and the packet framing where the radio matches a sync word \-\- and two boards set to the same framing pass a payload between them\. Expect the options and the output to change\.
.P
The CC2400 on an Ubertooth is a plain 2\.4 GHz radio, and Bluetooth is only what the firmware usually asks it to do\. The generic receive hands that choice to you: it programs no demodulator of its own and receives with whatever configuration is already in the radio's registers\. So a 2\.4 GHz protocol that is not Bluetooth can be captured without new firmware, as long as the CC2400 can be set up for it\.
.P
Two commands do it\. \fBubertooth\-debug \-w\fR writes the registers, and \fBubertooth\-dump \-g\fR starts the capture:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-debug \-w %manand=0x7fff,%lmtst=0x2b22,%mdmtst0=0x134b,%grmdm=0x0101,%fsdiv=2401,%mdmctrl=0x0029
ubertooth\-dump \-g \-b \-d symbols\.txt
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
One \fB\-w\fR takes the whole list, so the registers go in one of them\.
.P
That example is Bluetooth Basic Rate, as a case you can check against \fBubertooth\-dump \-c\fR: GFSK at 160 kHz deviation (MDMCTRL \fB0x0029\fR) on 2402 MHz (FSDIV is the frequency in MHz less the 1 MHz intermediate frequency), with GRMDM \fB0x0101\fR for un\-buffered mode and no packet framing\. The board streams demodulated symbols and \fB\-b\fR prints them as \fB0\fR and \fB1\fR\.
.P
The registers that matter, and what each decides:
.IP "\(bu" 4
MDMCTRL : the frequency deviation, which is the data rate the demodulator expects\. \fB0x0029\fR is 160 kHz, \fB0x0040\fR is 250 kHz\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
FSDIV : the channel, as the frequency in MHz less 1 for the intermediate frequency\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
GRMDM : buffered or un\-buffered, packet mode or not, how many preamble bytes, how much of the sync word to match, and whether the radio checks a CRC\. Its packet\-mode bit is what the board reads to choose between the two receives below\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
SYNCH and SYNCL : the sync word, in packet mode\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
MANAND, LMTST and MDMTST0 : the analog and test settings the Bluetooth receives use; a protocol at a different rate may want others\.
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBubertooth\-debug \-r\fR reads any of them back, and the CC2400 datasheet is where the fields come from\. Nothing in these tools knows which values make sense together: a configuration that does not work gives a capture with nothing in it, not an error\.
.P
With GRMDM's packet\-mode bit set, the board waits for the sync word instead, and sends one record each time the radio reports one: the four sync\-word bytes, then a fixed 32 bytes read out of the radio's FIFO\. It reads no length and waits for no end of packet, so a record is a sample of the FIFO and not necessarily one whole packet\. It keeps waiting on a quiet channel, answering requests, until the capture is stopped\.
.P
That path asks more of the configuration than the symbol stream does, because the radio does the framing: the preamble length, how much of the sync word it matches and its CRC choice all have to match what is on the air, and a choice that does not gives a capture with no records in it rather than a message\.
.P
The configuration lives in the radio and nothing stores it, so write the registers and start the capture as two commands with nothing in between\. A mode ending takes the radio through its own reset, which clears what \fB\-w\fR wrote; the board being idle is what keeps it\. \fBubertooth\-util \-S\fR leaves an idle board, so a run that is stopped properly can be set up again and restarted\.
.SS "A generic packet between two boards"
\fBubertooth\-tx \-g\fR transmits one generic packet, and that is the way to see the packet\-framing receive above work: it is the one transmitter whose framing is known\. The board frames the packet in the radio \-\- 6 preamble bytes of \fB01010101\fR, the whole 32\-bit sync word, no CRC, which is GRMDM \fB0x0f61\fR \-\- so the receiving board has to be set to the same value and the same sync word\.
.P
Two boards, the first receiving on 2402 MHz and the second sending it eight counted bytes ten times:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-debug \-U 0 \-w %manand=0x7fff,%lmtst=0x2b22,%mdmtst0=0x134b,%grmdm=0x0f61,%mdmctrl=0x0057,%synch=0xcb87,%syncl=0x6543,%fsdiv=2401
ubertooth\-dump \-U 0 \-g \-d packets\.bin &
ubertooth\-util \-U 1 \-a 1
ubertooth\-tx \-U 1 \-g 0102030405060708 \-a cb876543 \-c 2402 \-n 10
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
What has to agree between the two, and what does not:
.IP "\(bu" 4
GRMDM, exactly: \fB0x0f61\fR at the receiving end is the value the transmit programs, so the preamble, the part of the sync word matched, and the CRC choice all match\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
the sync word: \fB\-a cb876543\fR is SYNCH \fB0xcb87\fR and SYNCL \fB0x6543\fR\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
the channel, less 1 MHz at the receiving end: a transmit tunes the carrier directly and a receive subtracts its 1 MHz intermediate frequency, so \fB\-c 2402\fR goes with FSDIV \fB2401\fR\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
the deviation: MDMCTRL \fB0x0057\fR, which is what the firmware writes for the packet as it stands\. It is wider than any of the Bluetooth receives use, and writing it on both boards is the recipe that works whether or not the firmware goes on setting it for the packet\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
the power: \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR sets it, and two boards side by side at the default 7 are too loud for each other \-\- the receiving board reads nothing at all\. Turn the transmitter down to 0 or 1 for a short path, or put an attenuator in front of the receiver\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Each record in \fBpackets\.bin\fR then holds the sync word the radio matched and the payload that followed it; ubertooth\-dump(1), THE GENERIC RECEIVE says where in the record they are, so a counted payload can be checked against what was sent\. The receive reads a fixed 32 bytes, so keep a payload that is meant to be read back whole to 32 bytes or fewer\.
.SH "OUTPUT AND DIAGNOSTICS"
Every tool follows this split\. Each tool's own page gives the lines it prints and the statuses it exits with, under DIAGNOSTICS, EXIT STATUS, or both\.
.P
Standard output carries only what was asked for: the report a query returns, the packets or samples a capture decodes, the capture data itself when the file argument is \fB\-\fR, and the usage text for \fB\-h\fR\. Everything else goes to standard error, so a script reading a tool's standard output is not fed a diagnostic or a progress line\.
.P
Errors are one line starting \fBerror:\fR; warnings start \fBwarning:\fR and notes start \fBnote:\fR\. Progress and status ("Resetting \|\.\|\.\|\.", "Ubertooth stopped", DFU progress) carry no prefix\. The usage text goes to standard output for \fB\-h\fR, and to standard error after the \fBerror:\fR line when a tool that needs arguments is run with none (a tool with work to do and no options, such as ubertooth\-specan(1), runs instead)\. Every other refused command line is the \fBerror:\fR line alone: it describes the problem, so \fB\-h\fR is where the usage text comes from\.
.P
Exit status is 0 only when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and 1 otherwise: a usage error, a command line with nothing to do, a device that is missing or failed, a partial failure\. ubertooth\-afh(1) also uses 2, for a detection phase that received no packet\.
.P
One warning is worth knowing on sight\. When a capture ends, the USB transfer it was reading into has to come back before the device can be closed; a board that never returns it leaves the tool no safe way to close the device, so it says \fBwarning: the receive transfer did not finish; the Ubertooth is left open and may still be capturing\fR, asks that board for nothing further, and exits 1: the capture lost its stream, so it holds less than was asked for\. The board usually needs \fBubertooth\-util [\-U <selector>] \-S\fR or \fB\-r\fR afterwards, and one that answers neither usually resets itself about three seconds later and comes back idle\.
.P
A capture that is losing packets says so, whichever way it reads them\. A receive transfer that arrived with part of a packet cannot be read as a record, so it is dropped, and the capture reports it once per run, because such a fault usually repeats: \fBwarning: a receive transfer arrived with 32 of a packet's 64 bytes and was dropped; the capture is losing packets\fR\. That is the path \fBubertooth\-dump\fR, \fBubertooth\-rx\fR, \fBubertooth\-scan\fR, \fBubertooth\-afh\fR, \fBubertooth\-follow\fR and \fBubertooth\-specan\fR read through\. \fBubertooth\-btle\fR and \fBubertooth\-ego\fR poll for their packets one at a time instead, and report the same fault in the same way: \fBwarning: a poll reply arrived with 32 of a packet's 64 bytes and was dropped; the capture is losing packets\fR\. The short replies a board sends on purpose are not reported: the keep\-alive a quiet streaming capture gets, a single byte, and the one\-byte "nothing queued" answer every poll of a quiet capture gets\. The capture runs on and ends as it otherwise would, so the warning is the only sign: it says packets were lost, it does not fail the run\.
.P
A capture also says when its receiver is being driven harder than it can measure: \fBwarning: the signal is at the top of the receiver's range, so the board cannot measure it and may not demodulate it; the boards may be too close together, or the transmit power too high\fR\. Said once per capture, with a total when the capture ends: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 stretches where the signal was at the top of the receiver's range\fR, one per block of records judged to be there\.
.P
The signal figure a record carries comes from the radio on a scale that runs out: above a certain reading the host can only say "at least this loud", which it reports as 0 dBm, and a receiver held there is also past where it demodulates reliably\. Every receive that captures turns the board's amplifier on and the Basic Rate and low\-energy ones turn its high\-gain mode on too, which is what makes the near end of a bench reach it\. On the bench this measures as two separate regimes rather than a borderline: a transmitting board a few centimeters from the receiver holds every record at the top of the scale at every one of the eight transmit power levels, while the same board a meter away behind a 20 dB attenuator stays well below it even at level 7\. So where the link is two boards of your own, the warning is about the link rather than the setting of any one option: put more distance or more attenuation between them, or turn the transmit power down, and the capture has a signal it can both measure and read\. Anything else close and loud enough will do it too, and the board cannot say what is deafening it \-\- only that something is\.
.P
One packet at the top of the scale is not this, and is not reported: a short packet from a board a few centimeters away arrives with \fBrssi=0\fR and a valid CRC\. What is reported is a receiver held there, which is judged over a block of records rather than one\.
.P
Only the captures whose records are samples of the air report it, and that is the point rather than a limitation\. The Basic Rate stream sends a record for every block of air it collects, packet or no packet, so \fBubertooth\-rx\fR, \fBubertooth\-dump\fR with \fB\-c\fR or \fB\-l\fR (which is every run that does not ask for \fB\-g\fR), \fBubertooth\-scan\fR, \fBubertooth\-afh\fR and \fBubertooth\-follow\fR notice a receiver that is pinned even while they decode nothing at all \-\- which is the case worth knowing about, since that is what a deafened receiver looks like\.
.P
ubertooth\-btle(1) does not report it, under any of \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR and \fB\-p\fR\. Its board sends a record for a packet it demodulated and for nothing else, so a record at the top of the range there says a packet decoded at the top of the range, which an ordinary close link produces all day: a short packet from a board a few centimeters away arrives at the ceiling with a valid CRC\. Judging such records would warn about captures that are working, and the case that matters \-\- a link so loud that nothing decodes \-\- sends that tool no records to judge at all\. Run ubertooth\-rx(1) on the same channel to tell a deafened receiver from an empty one\.
.P
The generic receive (\fBubertooth\-dump \-g\fR) reports it or not according to which of its two receives the radio's configuration selects, which GENERIC RADIO MODE above describes: with GRMDM's packet\-mode bit clear it reads a stream of symbols through the same path as a Basic Rate capture, takes a reading per poll and reports this as that capture does, while with the bit set the board waits on a sync word, takes no readings at all, and its records state no signal figure for anything to judge\.
.P
ubertooth\-ego(1) never reports it: its records state no signal figure either, and its receive leaves the high\-gain mode off in any case\. ubertooth\-specan(1) is left out for a different reason: its sweep also reads the radio with the high\-gain mode off and reports the raw readings themselves, so its figures are not on the scale this warning is about\. The \fBbtbr\fR image's own tools report nothing here at all, because that firmware sends none of these records\.
.P
libubertooth follows the split already: it returns what it reads to the tool that called it and reports its own problems on standard error, so a library message does not land in the middle of a capture\. The clock trim and AFH channel notes raised while following a piconet are \fBnote:\fR lines on standard error\. What the library still writes to standard output is the calling tool's result \(em the decoded packet reports, the AFH map of \fBubertooth\-afh\fR, the version line \(em which is the right stream for them, whichever layer prints them\. libbtbb writes there too, while it recovers a piconet's address and about a packet it could not use\. The channel map the two BlueZ tools report is the exception: those tools print it themselves, so no library writes it\. Its device\-selection messages are \fBerror:\fR lines on standard error, each with whatever detail it needs under it: the boards listed after a refusal for several attached, and the counts and reason after a serial that matched nothing\.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
\fBUBERTOOTH_ALLOW_API_MISMATCH\fR decides what a tool does about firmware of another API version, whatever the build it came from\. \fB1\fR warns and runs the command, which is how a release build is made to talk to firmware from another commit for debugging; \fB0\fR refuses it, which is how a development build is made strict, for a test harness that wants a refusal rather than a warning\. Only those two exact values decide: unset, empty or anything else leaves the build's own answer, so a typo cannot turn the guard off\. See FIRMWARE OF ANOTHER API VERSION above\.
.P
\fBUBERTOOTH_TEST_NO_DEVICES=1\fR makes every tool behave as if no Ubertooth were attached: nothing is listed or opened on the USB bus, and the tool reports that it found no device\. It exists for the project's own test suite, which runs the tools while boards may be in use elsewhere, and it is the only setting that changes device selection\. Only the exact value \fB1\fR turns it on; unset, empty or any other value leaves device selection alone\. A tool that finds no board because of it says so: \fBnote: UBERTOOTH_TEST_NO_DEVICES is set, so no board is selected\fR\.
